{{!-- META {"path":"header-footer-sidebars","title":"Header, footer and sidebars","description":"The five chrome slots that wrap every page, where each one renders, how to edit them, and the rules their markup follows.","parent":"concepts","order":4,"data":{}} --}}
{{> page-hero parent=page.parent}}
<div class="ws-docs">
  {{> docs-nav}}
  <article class="ws-article">
    <p>The header, the footer and anything else that appears on every page live in <strong>slots</strong>: five fields in the project's website settings, rendered around each page's body. They are not pages and not templates. A page called "Header" fills no slot; the slot is where that markup goes.</p>

    <h2 id="slots">The five slots</h2>
    <p>The page skeleton owns the landmark elements and wraps each slot in one, with a fixed id. A slot's wrapper is only written when the slot has content.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>Setting</th><th>Wrapped in</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Main Navigation</td><td><code>website.mainNav</code></td><td><code>nav#main-nav</code></td><td>The header: desktop bar and mobile drawer, at the top of the body.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><em>(the page)</em></td><td>page source</td><td><code>main#page-content</code></td><td>The page's own content.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Left Sidebar</td><td><code>website.sidebarLeft</code></td><td><code>aside#sidebar-left</code></td><td>Site-wide side content, written after the body.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Right Sidebar</td><td><code>website.sidebarRight</code></td><td><code>aside#sidebar-right</code></td><td>The same, on the other side.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Footer</td><td><code>website.footer</code></td><td><code>footer#footer</code></td><td>The footer, below the body and the sidebars.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bottom</td><td><code>website.bottom</code></td><td><code>div#bottom</code></td><td>Global dialogs and schema.org microdata, after the footer. Usually invisible.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The same settings hold three parts that are not slots: <strong>Critical CSS</strong>, inlined in the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> of every page after the brand tokens; <strong>Head HTML</strong>, raw HTML for meta and site-verification tags; and <strong>Scripts</strong>, raw HTML inserted at the end of the body. Analytics does not belong in either raw part: connect it under Analytics, where it waits for visitor consent.</p>

    <h2 id="editing">Editing a slot</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Website Settings → Document skeleton</strong> shows every part as a map of the document, in render order, with its size. The header, sidebars and footer open in the full editor, with a live preview, device widths and click-to-code. Bottom and the raw parts open in the code editor.</li>
      <li>In any page editor, hover the header, footer or a sidebar in the preview and click its edit button.</li>
      <li>The Main Navigation and Footer editors have <strong>Insert the default navigation</strong> and <strong>Insert the default footer</strong>, which put the built-in recipes back.</li>
      <li>Over the API or MCP, slots are fields of the <code>settings</code> entity. Patch one field with a merge write, so the other slots stay untouched.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="ws-browser">
      <div class="ws-browser-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span class="ws-browser-url">Website Settings · Document skeleton</span></div>
      {{> ws-media slot="doc_chrome_skeleton" class="rounded-none"}}
    </figure>

    <h2 id="rules">Markup rules</h2>
    <p>A slot is checked when you save it, with the same rules as a page. A broken slot is refused with its name in the message, instead of disappearing from every page later.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inner markup only.</strong> Don't write <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code>; the skeleton already provides them. Use <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>. daisyUI's <code>navbar</code>, <code>menu</code> and <code>footer</code> classes work on any element.</li>
      <li><strong>No snippets.</strong> Slots render without partials, so <code>\{{&gt; name}}</code> fails here. Put the markup in the slot itself.</li>
      <li><strong>The usual template rules:</strong> no inline event handlers, no raw triple-brace output, and every bound <code>href</code> or <code>src</code> through <code>\{{sw-url …}}</code>. An unknown helper is refused at save, because a typo in a slot would show on every page.</li>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> up to 256 KB per slot.</li>
      <li><strong>Readable code:</strong> slots are stored exactly as written and shared by every page, so keep them indented. Minifying happens at build time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="context">What a slot can read</h2>
    <p>Each slot renders once per page, with that page's context. <code>\{{page.title}}</code> is the page being viewed, and <code>\{{sw-active path}}</code> marks the current item in a menu. Slots can read <code>company</code>, <code>website</code>, <code>page</code>, <code>pages</code>, <code>dataset</code> and the menus in <code>nav</code>:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Binding</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>\{{#each nav.header}}</code></td><td>Pages in the main navigation, with their dropdown <code>children</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>\{{#each nav.mobile}}</code></td><td>Pages in the mobile menu</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>\{{#each nav.footer}}</code></td><td>Pages in the footer menu</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>\{{#each nav.custom}}</code></td><td>Pages in the custom slot, for a list of your own</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>\{{#each company.social}}</code></td><td>Social profiles from Corporate Identity: <code>link</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>icon</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>\{{sw-theme-toggle}}</code></td><td>A light and dark switch; renders nothing until themes are enabled</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>\{{#each page.translations}}</code></td><td>The current page in other languages, for a language switcher</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>How pages get into those menus, and in which order, is covered in <a href="{{sw-url '/docs/concepts/pages-and-routes'}}">Pages and routes</a>.</p>

