<span class="tk-tag">&lt;/div&gt;</span></code></pre>
    </div>
    <h3 id="editable">Editable text in a slot</h3>
    <p>A slot is not a page, so it has no <code>page.data</code> of its own. Two kinds of key make slot text editable in the Content Editor:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>data-sw-text="website.data.footer_tagline"</code> (or <code>data-sw-html</code> for rich text) stores one value in the site-wide <code>website.data</code>. Edit it once and every page shows it.</li>
      <li><code>data-sw-translate="footer_cta"</code> stores a project translation: one value per language, shared by every page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="header">The header</h2>
    <p>New projects start with the <code>nav-header</code> recipe in the Main Navigation slot: a desktop bar with hover dropdowns, a mobile drawer, a language switcher that appears once there is a second language, and the theme toggle. Treat it as the mechanics and give it your own look.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The mobile drawer</strong> is CSS only: a hidden checkbox with the id <code>sw-nav-drawer</code>, a label as the menu button, a second label as the backdrop. Keep that id. While it is checked the platform lifts the header above other floating elements, and tapping an in-page link closes it. The menu button and the drawer must be in the same slot.</li>
      <li><strong>Sticky header:</strong> Website Settings → Nav, buttons &amp; preloader offers Pinned (always visible) or Hide on scroll down. The platform pads the page so content clears the bar, and sets <code>html.sw-scrolled</code> once the page has scrolled, for your own styling.</li>
      <li><strong>A header of a different height</strong> must set <code>--sw-header-h</code> in Critical CSS at every breakpoint where its height changes. The default is <code>4.5rem</code>, and <code>4.75rem</code> from 1024 pixels up. Jump links and the sticky offset read it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="sidebars">Sidebars</h2>
    <p>The sidebar slots are written <strong>after</strong> the page body in the document and are not a layout grid. Position them with their own classes, for example a fixed side tab or a floating contact button, so they do not push the page content around. For a sidebar that belongs to one page's layout, such as a table of contents beside an article, use a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> in that page instead. This documentation's menu is built that way.</p>

    <h2 id="bottom">The Bottom slot and global dialogs</h2>
    <p>A <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> placed in the Bottom slot exists on every page. A menu item whose target is <code>#newsletter</code> opens it from any menu, and a link to <code>#newsletter</code> does the same.</p>
    <div class="ws-window overflow-hidden rounded-2xl">
      <div class="ws-dots flex items-center gap-1.5 border-b border-white/10 px-4 py-3"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span class="ml-3 font-code text-xs text-white/50">website.bottom</span></div>
<pre class="overflow-x-auto"><code><span class="tk-tag">&lt;dialog</span> <span class="tk-attr">id</span>=<span class="tk-str">"newsletter"</span> <span class="tk-attr">class</span>=<span class="tk-str">"modal"</span><span class="tk-tag">&gt;</span>
  <span class="tk-tag">&lt;div</span> <span class="tk-attr">class</span>=<span class="tk-str">"modal-box"</span><span class="tk-tag">&gt;</span>
    <span class="tk-tag">&lt;h3</span> <span class="tk-attr">class</span>=<span class="tk-str">"text-lg font-bold"</span> <span class="tk-attr">data-sw-translate</span>=<span class="tk-str">"newsletter_title"</span><span class="tk-tag">&gt;</span>Stay in the loop<span class="tk-tag">&lt;/h3&gt;</span>
    <span class="tk-tag">&lt;p</span> <span class="tk-attr">class</span>=<span class="tk-str">"py-4"</span> <span class="tk-attr">data-sw-translate</span>=<span class="tk-str">"newsletter_text"</span><span class="tk-tag">&gt;</span>One email a month.<span class="tk-tag">&lt;/p&gt;</span>
    <span class="tk-tag">&lt;form</span> <span class="tk-attr">method</span>=<span class="tk-str">"dialog"</span><span class="tk-tag">&gt;&lt;button</span> <span class="tk-attr">class</span>=<span class="tk-str">"btn"</span><span class="tk-tag">&gt;</span>Close<span class="tk-tag">&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/form&gt;</span>
  <span class="tk-tag">&lt;/div&gt;</span>
<span class="tk-tag">&lt;/dialog&gt;</span></code></pre>
    </div>
    <div class="ws-callout"><p class="!mt-0"><strong>Structured data.</strong> A <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;</code> block does not belong in a slot. Once the business details in Corporate Identity are filled in, the platform writes schema.org JSON-LD into the <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> from them, including the business type you choose there.</p></div>
  </article>
